A||N||8||Will||Early and '88 rack bushings||||1||
Z||000000||Will||09-24-2000||05:50 PM||||Are '88 rack bushings interchangeable with '84-'87 rack bushings?<BR>     I think mine are in need of replacement, and I have an early rack out back in the parts car. Can I just switch them?<BR>     I've heard that changing them is a PITA. Any advice on how to do this?||||reg||1
Z||000001||gixxer||09-24-2000||06:33 PM||||Just replaced my march of '88' built rack bushing a couple weeks ago. It was the later style:<BR>a plastic snap in jobber. The earlier racks used a bush held in with a snap ring, I think. The stocker was replaced using Mr. Dickmans kit, which I liked.<BR>You could probably replace the bush in the car, but you may end up tweaking the rack.<BR>Yeah it's a PITA the first time. If I ever have to take the rack out again, tho, I don't think it'll be any problem at all.  <BR>     ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Will||09-24-2000||08:13 PM||||To be continued here:<BR> <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/005882.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/005882.html</A> <P>Thanks for the info about Rodney. I hadn't realized he had proliferated that much. For &lt; $20 it's a toss up between buying his (aluminum), or making one of my own (possibly copying his--for only my own use, of course) out of a more suitable material (oilite bronze, for instance).<P>Is Rodney here? Why do you make those bushings out of aluminum? That's not really a good bearing material.<p>[This message has been edited by Will (edited 09-24-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||Rodney||09-24-2000||08:49 PM||||I buy these rack bushings from the largest steering rack wholesale parts supplier in the US who supplies these to rack rebuilders. That is what they use. I could have them made but the liability issue makes buying them from someone else my only real option. ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Rodney||09-24-2000||08:49 PM||||I buy these rack bushings from the largest steering rack wholesale parts supplier in the US who supplies these to rack rebuilders. That is what they use. I could have them made but the liability issue makes buying them from someone else my only real option. ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Ozzy||09-24-2000||10:19 PM||||Personally, I'd recommend buying Rodney's replacement. Unless you have a lathe and the materials for nothing it's not worth it. My '88 Coupe had the same problem and I thought the same thing...I think I also asked the same question about an alloy replacement; I bought the one Rodney sells and it worked very well and was very easy to install. I've since sold the Coupe but put a lot of miles on it with no problem. I was also surprised at the tolerances. They were spot on.  He shipped it to me even before he got my check!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Go with what works...especially when your only talking $20.00 or so!<P>Ozzy<p>[This message has been edited by Ozzy (edited 09-24-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||Will||09-24-2000||10:48 PM||||Rodney:<BR>     Please don't take my post the wrong way. I think you are doing the Fiero community a great service, and I've heard nothing but good about your business.<BR>     I was just curious about why they are the way they are, but if you don't make them, I guess I can't hold it against you, can I?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><BR>     I'm concerned about their suitability because my rack was abused before I got it. In my first attempt to fix the steering problem I am still trying to fix, I took off the locknut and adjuster plug from the preload adjustment above the pinion. I found that the rack bearing there was completely worn out (The grease groove that is supposed to be there was worn away flush), with only the dried up remains of factory grease lubricating it. The rack wasn't too bad, so I greased it, replaced the bearing, and added a grease fitting on top of the adjuster plug. It wouldn't surprise me at all if the end bushing were worn out. I know, if given my dithers, I would replace it with a material meant to be a bearing, but I guess I can't argue with something that works.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Will||09-24-2000||10:59 PM||||Ozzy:<BR>     I can get lathe time for free. Enough SAE 841 oil impregnated bronze to do the job can be had from McMaster-Carr &lt;www.mcmaster.com&gt; for $30 or less.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000008||Ozzy||09-25-2000||01:56 AM||||Kool...personally, I think the aluminum bushing will out last your rack but do as you feel...<P>The other option is to replace the whole rack with the Fiero Stores reman. rack with brass bushings fitted with grease nimples. They are around $220.00 (approx.) with core exchange. I would have done this but the only problem on my rack was the worn passenger side bushing.<P>Ozzy||||reg||1
